  "name": "A Study of the Ocean Migrations of Sockeye Salmon and Estimation of the Carrying-Capacity of the North Pacific Ocean Using a Dynamical Salmon Ecosystem Model (NOPASA)",
  "description": "This report describes the use of NOAPASA to determine the average seasonal movements of salmon in the ocean as affected by currents and water temperature, and to determine weather the ocean food resources might be limiting salmon production. The North Pacific Salmon simulation model (NOPASA) is a numerical simulation, based on available quantitative data and knowledge of processes. The NOPASA I deals mainly with sockeye, although the carrying capacity of the North Pacific is computed using all species of Pacific salmon. [Reference: FAVORITE, F., and T. LAEVASTU. 1979. A study of the ocean migrations of sockeye salmon and estimation of the carrying-capacity of the North Pacific Ocean using a Dynamical Salmon Ecosystem Model (NOPASA). NWAFC Processed Rep. 79-16, 47 p. Northwest and Alaska Fish. Cent., Natl. Mar. Fish. Serv., NOAA, 7600 Sand Point Way NE, Seattle, WA 98115-0070.]\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: To simulate in space and time of oceanic migrations of sockeye salmon.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23563676",
